course: dictionary
lesson: 5926
title: Marsh and wetland words
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A marsh is low, flat land that is wet and soft most of the time. It has tall grasses and reeds growing in it, and many birds, frogs and insects live there. «Long legs let cranes wade in marshes and tall grass» tells you a marsh is wet enough to stand in, but not deep water.
explain: Look at the words near marsh to help you. «Wetlands such as marshes and swamps soak up flood water like a sponge» tells you a marsh is a wetland, which is land soaked with water for much of the year. A swamp is different, because its trees stand in water. Check the clues before you decide.
explain: Marsh also appears in phrases. A «salt marsh» is a marsh with salty water, and «marsh gas» is a gas that comes up from the mud. Words that go together can help you work out the meaning. Always try your meaning in the sentence to see if it fits.
fact: A marsh is low, flat land that is wet and soft most of the time.
fact: A marsh is a kind of wetland, full of reeds and grasses, and home to birds, frogs and insects.
fact: A «salt marsh» is a marsh with salty water.
fact: «Marsh gas» is a gas that comes up from the mud when dead plants rot in airless mud.
fact: A marsh is not the same as a swamp: a swamp has trees standing in water.
exercise: «Long legs let cranes wade in marshes and tall grass.» Where do the cranes wade? | In a hot desert of sand | On a high, dry mountain | *In wet, soft land with reeds | In a swimming pool with tiles
exercise: «A marsh is full of reeds and grasses, a swamp has trees standing in water.» What grows in a marsh? | Tall trees standing in deep water | *Reeds and grasses | Ice and snow | Cactus and sand
exercise: «Poke it with a stick and stinky bubbles of marsh gas come up.» What comes up from marsh mud? | *Marsh gas | Clean water from a tap | Sand and pebbles | Dry dust
test: Nadia says a marsh is a kind of forest. Is she right? | No, a marsh is a high mountain | Yes, marshes are full of trees | *No, a marsh is wet land with reeds and grasses, and a forest is mostly trees | Yes, because marshes are in the sea
test: «Wetlands such as marshes and swamps soak up flood water like a sponge.» Why is this useful? | It turns the water into ice | It makes the water salty | It stops birds from flying | *It soaks up flood water, which can help stop floods getting worse
exam: Why should you stay on paths when you walk across a marsh? | Because marsh birds are always hungry | *Because marsh mud can be boggy and dangerous | Because the grass is too short to see | Because marshes are always dry and hard
